{
  "schema": "l2_set_conflict_associativity_v1",
  "mechanism": "l2_set_conflict_and_associativity",
  "hardware_id": "metax_c500",
  "allowed_use": "Evidence that a working set far inside the replacement capacity is retained in full at every address stride tested, so a regular stride cannot concentrate a grouped GEMM's operand lines onto few cache sets on this target.  Also fixes the constant downstream read every launch of this kernel pays, which must be removed before the passes are read.",
  "forbidden_use": "This is a null result about set conflict, not a retention model.  It must not be used to price any traffic, and it says nothing about working sets that exceed the replacement capacity, which the cyclic reuse probe covers instead.",
  "production_or_evaluation_duration_consumed": false,
  "instrument": "mcProfiler perf_exec --custom --per-kernel, one marker window per part, one numbered per-invocation file per ordinal",
  "counters": [
    "Global Memory Read bytes",
    "L2C Hit Rate"
  ],
  "geometry": {
    "block_threads": 256,
    "line_bytes": 128,
    "passes": 8,
    "workgroups": 1,
    "note": "One workgroup cycles over `lines` cache lines spaced `stride_bytes` apart, eight times.  The footprint is at most 64 KiB, two orders of magnitude inside the replacement capacity, so a fully associative cache of this size serves every pass after the first from cache at every stride.  A single workgroup keeps the rest of the chip from displacing the working set, and the downstream read counter is not reached by vector-cache hits, so L1 cannot mask the effect."
  },
  "why_independent_axes": "The shared miss core is a fully associative byte-capacity LRU.  A real L2 is set associative, so a working set that fits by capacity can still thrash when more lines than the associativity map to one set, and a grouped GEMM walks its operands at large regular strides.  The strides are read off the production launch geometry -- 5760 bytes is the activation row stride, the K extent times the element size -- never off a prediction error.",
  "finding": "Every launch of this kernel pays a constant downstream read of about 2880 bytes, 22.5 lines, identical across all 72 configurations.  After removing it, the realised passes are 1.000 everywhere: strides of 128 bytes, 4, 16, 64 and 256 KiB, 1 MiB, and the two non-power-of-two strides 5760 and 196608 bytes, at line counts from 2 to 512, deviate from one full compulsory pass by at most 0.008.  Six power-of-two strides give bit-identical numbers that depend only on the line count.  No set conflict exists at any tested stride: either the associativity exceeds 512 ways or the set index is hashed, and in both cases a regular stride cannot concentrate on a set.",
  "build": "/opt/maca/mxgpu_llvm/bin/mxcc -x maca -offload-arch native --maca-path=/opt/maca -O3 -o probe l2_set_conflict_associativity.cpp",
  "tool_limitation": "mcProfiler stops emitting per-kernel reports after about fifty kernels in one marker window, so the sweep is split by MIDLAYER_PROBE_PART; a first single-window run silently dropped its last 22 ordinals.  Its summary rendering also fails on this kernel with 'can not find export value @sl1_hit_rate', which does not affect the per-kernel reports.",
  "status": "candidate_pending_main_agent_review",
